
# Sandbox of Jieyun

# Correct way to use the code from my sandbox

* Suppose that *Basilisk* is installed in the directory $(BASILISK).

* During your code development, you should work in a separate directory,
for example **~/basilisk_dev_jieyun** (as in my case). This directory should
not contain any source code from the original Basilisk distribution, 
but only your own code. You are free to use any version control system
for your work, such as Git or Darcs.

* After downloading the code from my sandbox, place all the directories
and files under **~/basilisk_dev_jieyun**.
Next, add a **Makefile** in **~/basilisk_dev_jieyun/test**.
A basic Makefile should include at least the following two lines:

``` Makefile
include $(BASILISK)/Makefile.defs
CFLAGS += -I../src
```

The first line gives you access to the header files provided by Basilisk.
The second line instructs the qcc compiler to search for header files in your
local source directory, i.e. ~/basilisk_dev_jieyun/src.

* **DO NOT** add or overwrite any files in $(BASILISK).

* By following this approach, you will avoid contaminating the Basilisk
source code and maintain a clean separation between Basilisk and
your own development code.

# Edge-Based Interface-Tracking (EBIT) / Semushin method

The Edge-Based Interface Tracking (EBIT) method is a novel Front-Tracking method
developed by [Chirco and Zaleski, 2022](#chirco2022), [Pan et al., 2024](#pan2024).
In the EBIT method, the markers are located on the grid edges and
the interface can be reconstructed without storing the connectivity of the markers. This feature
allows almost automatic parallelization on a quad/octree grid.

## Publications

~~~bib
@article{chirco2022,
  title={An edge-based interface-tracking method for multiphase flows},
  author={Chirco, Leonardo and Zaleski St\'{e}phane},
  journal={Int J Numer Meth Fluids.},
  volume={95(3)},
  pages={491-497},
  year={2022},
  publisher={Wiley},
  doi={https://doi.org/10.1002/fld.5144}
}

@article{pan2024,
  title={An edge-based interface tracking ({EBIT}) method for multiphase-flow simulation with surface tension},
  author={Pan, Jieyun and Long, Tian and Chirco, Leonardo and Scardovelli, Ruben and Popinet St\'{e}phane and Zaleski St\'{e}phane},
  journal={Journal of Computational Physics},
  volume={508},
  pages={113016},
  year={2024},
  publisher={Elsevier},
  doi={https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jcp.2024.113016},
  pdf={https://arxiv.org/pdf/2309.00338}
}

@article{long2024,
  title={An Edge-based Interface Tracking ({EBIT}) method for multiphase flows with phase change},
  author={Long, Tian and Pan, Jieyun and Zaleski St\'{e}phane},
  journal={Journal of Computational Physics},
  volume={513},
  pages={113159},
  year={2024},
  publisher={Elsevier},
  doi={https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jcp.2024.113159},
  pdf={https://arxiv.org/pdf/2402.13677}
}
~~~

## Header files

The main header files can be found on the following pages:

* [2D Interface advection](src/ebit-2d.h)
* [2D Interface advection with an unsplit scheme](src/ebit-unsplit.h)
* [Two-phase flow with the EBIT method](src/two-phase-ebit.h)
* [Fitting functions](src/fitting-ebit.h)
* [Helper functions for EBIT method](src/utils-ebit.h)
* [EBIT method + AMR](src/ebit-tree.h)
* [3D Interface advection](src/ebit-3d.h)

